I agree that these techniques have been used throughout history.

Humans have always used narratives, symbols, rituals, education, advertising, propaganda, myths, slogans, and cultural traditions to shape how people interpret the world. Modern psychology, behavioral science, communications, and marketing have simply studied those processes more systematically.

Examples include:

* Education using memorable stories and analogies to teach concepts.
* Military organizations using narratives and traditions to build identity and cohesion.
* Brands using recurring stories and symbols to create emotional associations.
* Political movements using narratives to unify supporters or frame events.
* Religious traditions using stories, rituals, and parables to transmit values across generations.

Those are all examples of narratives influencing how people remember, interpret, and act.

At the same time, people are not blank slates. Persuasion has limits. Individuals interpret messages through their own experiences, relationships, and existing beliefs, and competing narratives are constantly interacting. That’s why the same story can inspire one person, leave another unchanged, and alienate a third.

One of the most interesting questions, in my view, isn’t whether narrative shapes cognition—that’s well supported—but what makes some narratives enduring. The ones that last often do more than persuade in the moment. They provide a framework that people continue to revisit and reinterpret as their lives change, allowing the same story to acquire new meaning over time.

That enduring quality is part of why stories remain such a powerful medium for transmitting ideas across generations.